problems.problem:
Does there exist a triangle $ABC$ satisfying the following two conditions:

(a) $\sin^2A + \sin^2B + \sin^2C = \cot A + \cot B + \cot C$;

(b) $S\ge a^2 - (b - c)^2$, where $S$ is the area of triangle $ABC$?

Given a parallelogram $ABCD$, let $E$ be a point on the side $BC$ and $F$ a point on the side $CD$ such that the triangles $ABE$ and $BCF$ have the same area. The diagonal $BD$ meets $AE$ at $M$ and $AF$ at $N$. Prove that:

I. there exists a triangle whose sides are equal to $BM, MN, ND$;

II. when $E, F$ vary so that the length of $MN$ decreases, the circumradius of this triangle also decreases.

There are $25$ towns in a country. Find the smallest $k$ for which one can set up two-way flight routes between these towns so that the following conditions are satisfied:

1) from each town there are direct routes to exactly $k$ other towns;

2) if two towns are not connected by a direct route, then there is a town which has direct routes to both of them.

Find the greatest real number $\alpha$ for which there exists an infinite sequence of integers $(a_n)_{n\ge1}$ satisfying the following conditions:

1) $a_n > 1997n$ for every positive integer $n$;

2) for every $n\ge 2$, $U_n\ge a_n^{\alpha}$, where $U_n = \gcd\{a_i + a_k \mid i + k = n\}$.

At a conference there are $n \geq 10$ people. It is known that:

I. each person knows at least $\left\lfloor\frac{n+2}{3}\right\rfloor$ other people;

II. for any two people $A$ and $B$ who do not know each other, there exist people $A(1), A(2), \ldots, A(k)$ such that $A$ knows $A(1)$, $A(i)$ knows $A(i+1)$ for each $i$, and $A(k)$ knows $B$;

III. there is no Hamiltonian path.

Prove that the people can be divided into two groups such that one group has a Hamiltonian cycle and the other consists of people no two of whom know each other.

Let $H$ be a convex polygon. Show that for every real number $a \in (0, 1)$ there exist $6$ distinct points $A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_6$ on the sides of $H$, in clockwise order, satisfying the conditions:

I. $\overrightarrow{A_1A_2} = \overrightarrow{A_5A_4} = a \cdot \overrightarrow{A_6A_3}$;

II. the lines $A_1A_2$ and $A_5A_4$ are equidistant from $A_6A_3$.

A positive integer $n_0$ is written on a blackboard. Two players $A$ and $B$ play the following game:

• moving alternately, each player deletes the number $n_k$ written on the blackboard and writes instead a number $n_{k+1}$ from the set $\left\{n_k-1, \left\lfloor\frac {n_k}3\right\rfloor\right\}$;

• player $A$ moves first, replacing $n_0$ with $n_1\in\left\{n_0-1, \left\lfloor\frac {n_0}3\right\rfloor\right\}$;

• the game ends when the number on the board is $0$, and the player who wrote it wins.

Find which player has a winning strategy in each of the following cases: a) $n_0=120$; b) $n_0=\frac {3^{2002}-1}2$; c) $n_0=\frac{3^{2002}+1}2$.

Let $m$ be a positive integer which has a prime divisor greater than $\sqrt {2m} +1$. Find the minimal positive integer $n$ for which there exists a finite set $S$ of distinct positive integers satisfying the following two conditions:

I. $m\leq x\leq n$ for all $x\in S$;

II. the product of all elements of $S$ is a perfect square.

Let $(x_n)_{n\ge1}$ be the sequence defined by $x_1 = 603$, $x_2 = 102$ and $$x_{n + 2} = x_{n + 1} + x_n + 2\sqrt {x_{n + 1} x_n - 2}\qquad (n \geq 1).$$ Show that:

(1) $x_n$ is a positive integer for every $n \geq 1$;

(2) there are infinitely many positive integers $n$ for which the decimal representation of $x_n$ ends with $2003$;

(3) there is no positive integer $n$ for which the decimal representation of $x_n$ ends with $2004$.

Let $p>3$ be a prime. Calculate:

a) $S=\sum_{k=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} \left(\left\lfloor\frac{2k^2}{p}\right\rfloor-2 \left\lfloor\frac{k^2}{p}\right\rfloor\right)$ if $p\equiv 1 \pmod 4$;

b) $T=\sum_{k=1}^{\frac{p-1}{2}} \left\lfloor\frac{k^2}{p}\right\rfloor$ if $p\equiv 1 \pmod 8$.

Let $A, B$ be two sets of positive real numbers such that $|A| = |B| =n$, $A \neq B$ and $S(A)=S(B)$, where $|X|$ is the number of elements and $S(X)$ is the sum of all elements of a set $X$. Prove that one can fill the unit squares of an $n\times n$ board with positive numbers and zeros such that:

a) the set of the row sums equals $A$;

b) the set of the column sums equals $B$;

c) there are at least $(n-1)^{2}+k$ zeros in the board, where $k=|A \cap B|$.

Consider an $m\times n$ rectangular grid with $m$ rows and $n$ columns. There are water fountains on some of the squares. A fountain can spray water onto any of its adjacent squares, or onto a square in the same column such that there is exactly one square between them. Find the minimum number of fountains such that every square can be sprayed, in the cases

a) $m=4$;

b) $m=3$.

Find the smallest positive integer $n$ for which there exist $n$ real numbers $a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n$ satisfying the three conditions

a. $a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_n>0$;

b. $a_1^3+a_2^3+\cdots+a_n^3<0$;

c. $a_1^5+a_2^5+\cdots+a_n^5>0$.

Given $16$ distinct real numbers $\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\ldots,\alpha_{16}$, for each polynomial $P$ let $$V(P)=P(\alpha_1)+P(\alpha_2)+\cdots+P(\alpha_{16}).$$ Prove that there is a monic polynomial $Q$ of degree $8$ such that:

i) $V(QP)=0$ for every polynomial $P$ with $\deg P<8$;

ii) $Q$ has $8$ real roots (counted with multiplicity).

Consider the three functions $$P(x) = (x^2-1)^{2023},\qquad Q(x) = (2x+1)^{14},\qquad R(x) = \left(2x+1+\frac 2x \right)^{34}.$$ Initially, we pick a set $S$ containing two of these functions, and we perform operations on it. The allowed operations are:

• take two functions $p,q \in S$ and add one of $p+q$, $p-q$, $pq$ to $S$;

• take a function $p \in S$ and add $p^k$ to $S$, for any positive integer $k$ of our choice;

• take a function $p \in S$ and a real number $t$, and add one of the functions $p+t$, $p-t$, $pt$ to $S$.

Show that no matter which two functions are picked initially, no finite sequence of operations on $S$ yields the third function.

Let $a, b$ be coprime positive integers with $b$ odd and $a>2$. The sequence $(x_n)$ is defined by $x_0=2$, $x_1=a$ and $x_{n+2}=ax_{n+1}+bx_n$ for $n \geq 0$. Prove that:

a) if $a$ is even, then there do not exist positive integers $m, n, p$ such that $\frac{x_m} {x_nx_p}$ is a positive integer;

b) if $a$ is odd, then there do not exist positive integers $m, n, p$ such that $mnp$ is even and $\frac{x_m} {x_nx_p}$ is a perfect square.

A garden is organized as a $2024\times 2024$ board, and three types of flowers are planted in it: roses, daisies and orchids. The planting must satisfy the following conditions:

(i) each cell is planted with at most one type of flower; some cells may be left empty;

(ii) for each planted cell $A$, there are exactly $3$ other planted cells in the same row or column as $A$ that are planted with flowers of types different from that of $A$;

(iii) each type of flower is planted in at least $1$ cell.

What is the maximal number of cells that can be planted?
